#{[17592186484211 :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 205 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186484211 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 205 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202+0 & 203+0 & 204+1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202+0 & 203+0 & 204+1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 202 & 203\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 203-6\\cdot 202) \\\\  & =-(812-1\\,212) \\\\  & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484213 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,|B|=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ 3 & -3 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,|C|=\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186484224 :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0\\cdot 6-1\\cdot 4)+3(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =30+8-60 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$"] [17592186484229 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & k & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 1-5\\cdot k)-k(-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)-1(-3\\cdot k-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =8-10k+33k+3k+24 \\\\  & =26k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 26k+32 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{16}{13}.$"] [17592186484245 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186484245 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=3$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-3){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-2){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484248 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1)+0+(-2)(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =-6-22 \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-28|=28.\n$$"] [17592186484251 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{-2t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}{{e}^{3t}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484254 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -8 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186484254 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -8 & 8\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484263 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =4\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =(4⟨2t,\\,1,\\,0⟩)\\times (8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩) \\\\  & =32\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =32\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-32\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{(4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{64\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484278 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,|B|=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,|C|=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186484286 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{vmatrix}=5,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186484286 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & (b-1)+1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & (b-1)+1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{vmatrix}}_{5}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+(2-8) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486378 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{cos}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486405 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(3) & =T(3)\\times N(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}⟨(-9-16),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}⟨-25,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486409 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,2t,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2t & 0 \\\\ -2t & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(0-0),1+4{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,0,1+4{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486413 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),-(0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486416 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=(0+\\sqrt{2})\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}-0)\\,j+0\\,k=⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =k\\times (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩| \\\\  & =|1+1| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486418 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,-1,5)$ is $p=i-j+5\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,-1,3⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i-j+5\\,k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}|-4-5-5| \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486424 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(4,0,4)$ is $p=⟨4,\\,0,\\,4⟩.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-2)$ and the principal normal $N(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-2)\\times N(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j-\\sqrt{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨4,0,4⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|4+0-4| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486428 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{10}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8t}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{4t}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(5)^{2}+(-4t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{25+16{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{20{t}^{2}+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{4{t}^{2}+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{4{t}^{2}+5}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}⟨2t,2,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -4t & 2t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -4t & 2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2t & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}⟨4t-4t,-(4{t}^{2}+5),-8{t}^{2}-10⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}⟨0,\\,-(4{t}^{2}+5),\\,-2(4{t}^{2}+5)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486433 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)),0-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486436 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486438 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\pi $ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,4,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\ N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\pi )=⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,4\\pi ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|0+12\\pi +0| \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486439 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 7-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35+12 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486440 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡5t+25{cos}^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t-0,-({cos}^{2}⁡5t+{sin}^{2}⁡5t),0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486450 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟨{t}^{2},\\sqrt{2}t,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}⟨2t,\\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}),-2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {t}^{2} & \\sqrt{2}t & 1 \\\\ 2t & \\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}) & -2t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}t & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}) & -2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}{t}^{2} & 1 \\\\ 2t & -2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}{t}^{2} & \\sqrt{2}t \\\\ 2t & \\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}),-(-2{t}^{3}-2t),(\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})-2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}-\\sqrt{2},2{t}^{3}+2t,-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{4}-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨-\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1),2t({t}^{2}+1),-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}t,-{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486451 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,1)$ is $p=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\,i+\\,j+\\,k))\\times (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(i+j-2\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=i+j-2\\,k=⟨1,1,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-2⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot z & =-1 \\\\ x+y-2z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186489198 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ z & \\sqrt{x} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[0(0)-(-1)(\\sqrt{x})]i-[0(0)-(-1)(z)]j+[0(\\sqrt{x})-0(z)]k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\,i-z\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{x},-z,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(-z)⟨0,1,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}⟩+⟨\\sqrt{x},-z,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-z,-\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{x}}⟩+⟨\\sqrt{x},-z,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{x},-2z,-\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{x}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(4,1,4) & =⟨\\sqrt{4},-2(4),-\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-8,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186489230 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3x} & 2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[0(2y)-0({e}^{3x})]i-[2(2y)-0(0)]j+[2({e}^{3x})-0(0)]k \\\\  & =0\\,i-4y\\,j+2{e}^{3x}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4y,2{e}^{3x}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(2x+3)⟨2,0,3{e}^{3x}⟩+⟨0,-4y,2{e}^{3x}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(2x+3),0,(6x+9){e}^{3x}⟩+⟨0,-4y,2{e}^{3x}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(2x+3),-4y,(6x+11){e}^{3x}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,-1,4) & =⟨2(2(0)+3),-4(-1),(6(0)+11){e}^{3(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186489265 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (4F+G) & =∇\\times (4F)+∇\\times G \\\\  & =4∇\\times F+∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y{e}^{x} & xz & y-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{e}^{x})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})]k \\\\  & =(1-x)\\,i-(0-0)\\,j+(z-{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\  & =(1-x)\\,i+(z-{e}^{x})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (4F+G) & =4(∇\\times F)+∇\\times G \\\\  & =4[(1-x)\\,i+(z-{e}^{x})\\,k]+z\\,i \\\\  & =4(1-x)\\,i+4(z-{e}^{x})\\,k+z\\,i \\\\  & =[4(1-x)+z]\\,i+4(z-{e}^{x})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (4F+G))(0,1,-1) & =[4(1-0)+(-1)]\\,i+4(-1-{e}^{0})\\,k \\\\  & =[4-1]\\,i+4(-1-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-8\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186489290 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{y} & 0 \\\\ x{e}^{y} & z & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{y}(0)-0(z)]i-[1(0)-0(x{e}^{y})]j+[1(z)-(-{e}^{y})(x{e}^{y})]k \\\\  & =0\\,i-0\\,j+(z+x{e}^{2y})\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,z+x{e}^{2y}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(x-{e}^{y})⟨-1,0,-x{e}^{y}⟩+⟨0,0,z+x{e}^{2y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}-x,0,x{e}^{2y}-{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩+⟨0,0,z+x{e}^{2y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}-x,0,2x{e}^{2y}+z-{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(2,0,-3) & =⟨{e}^{0}-2,0,2(2){e}^{2(0)}+(-3)-(2)^{2}{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186491984 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${a}_{11}=1,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186491989 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -21 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186493904 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ k & -3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 0)-(2\\cdot (-3)-k\\cdot 0)+(2\\cdot (-2)-k\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6-4-k \\\\  & =5-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 5-k & =0 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=5.$"] [17592186493949 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k)-k(5\\cdot 1-3\\cdot -4)+2(5k-(-2)(-4)) \\\\  & =-2-3k-k(5+12)+2(5k-8) \\\\  & =-2-3k-17k+10k-16 \\\\  & =-10k-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ -10k-18 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{9}{5}.$"] [17592186495114 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=1$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+2}(-2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+(1)^{2}\\,k)=\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+2\\,j+k) \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+2}[2(1)\\,i+(2-(1)^{2})\\,j+2(1)\\,k]=\\frac{1}{3}[2\\,i+j+2\\,k].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=1$ is\n\n$$\np=r(1)=-6(1)\\,i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+(1)^{3}\\,k=⟨-6,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =(\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+2\\,j+k))\\times (\\frac{1}{3}[2\\,i+j+2\\,k]) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(3\\,i+6\\,j-6\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=3\\cdot (T(1)\\times N(1))=i+2\\,j-2\\,k=⟨1,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-2⟩ & =⟨-6,3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-2⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+2\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot z & =-2 \\\\ x+2y-2z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186495131 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,3,2)$ is $p=i+3\\,j+2\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-1)$ and the principal normal $N(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-1)\\times N(-1) \\\\  & =k\\times i \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i+3\\,j+2\\,k)\\cdot j| \\\\  & =|0+3+0| \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186495157 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(5,2,1)$ is $p=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(3)$ and the principal normal $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3)\\times N(3) & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\times \\frac{1}{5}(4\\,i-3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(-25\\,k) \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(3)\\times N(3)=-k=⟨0,0,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩ & =⟨5,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(0)+z(-1) & =5(0)+2(0)+1(-1) \\\\ 0+0-z & =0+0-1 \\\\ z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186496273 :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,dt & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =(i+k)\\times (i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{2}{3})i-(1-1)j+(\\frac{2}{3}-0)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186497386 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(2{e}^{x}){({e}^{2x})}^{′}-({e}^{2x}){(2{e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =(2{e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{2x})(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={4}^{3x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186502096 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-1 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(3,1)} & =(3{u}^{2}-1)\\,{|}_{u=3\\,v=1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186503685 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"] [17592186503712 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186503749 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n7{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 4.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(1)+7(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$"] [17592186504059 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504085 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504100 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504102 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504139 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504161 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504267 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504298 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504301 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504325 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504329 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504335 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504391 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504412 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504428 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186504625 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"] [17592186504625 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186504635 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"] [17592186504680 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite"] [17592186504680 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186504690 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186504696 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186504709 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"] [17592186505203 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505287 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505296 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505323 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505332 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505337 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505342 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505434 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505462 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505589 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186505739 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186505739 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ Computing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{22}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186505754 :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z\\}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n\nII. If $Tx=[1,-2,3]^{T}$ has no solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nIII. If $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\},\n$$ then $T$ is invertible"] [17592186505754 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z,$ any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $$\n\\displaystyle Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has no solution, this means that $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$ Therefore, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186505766 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186505766 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186506573 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)-8 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{2}(t)+9,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{3}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186506573 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =3\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}-2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =3{e}^{2\\cdot t}-2{e}^{3\\cdot t}+1 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{3t}+3{e}^{2t}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{3}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\cdot t}-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{3t}+3{e}^{2t}+1 \\\\ {e}^{3t}-3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186506578 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=-\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{3}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186506578 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\cdot t}-1 \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{3}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+3\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2\\cdot t}+1+3{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{4t}-{e}^{2t}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 3{e}^{4t}-{e}^{2t}+1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186507441 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3s-8}{(s-2)(s-3)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186507462 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186507472 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-2},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-2}-\\frac{4}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186507472 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\cdot t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}-\\frac{4}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}-4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot t}-4{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}-4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-4{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186507519 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186508990 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matric $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186509457 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $a+(4b-4c).$"] [17592186509457 :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(4b-4c) & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186509465 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $3a+(2b-(-5)c).$"] [17592186509465 :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+(2b-(-5)c) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -60\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
